  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through Sunday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: A trough of low pressure will pass\nnortheastward across the Silver State this afternoon into this\nevening, resulting in isolated to scattered thunderstorms in\nEastern Nevada. A few of these thunderstorms could be strong. The\nprimary thunderstorm hazards will be wind gusts near 55 mph and\ndangerous cloud to ground lightning strikes. A cooling trend is\nexpected Tuesday and Wednesday. By Wednesday afternoon, high\ntemperatures throughout Northern and Central Nevada will be seven\nto nine degrees below normal for this time of year. \n\nAn upper level area of low pressure will make landfall along the\ncoast of the Pacific Northwest Thursday. This cyclone aloft will\nmeander around the coast of the Pacific Northwest Friday into the\nupcoming weekend. Meanwhile, a tropical cyclone will be located in\nthe Eastern Pacific, just west of the Baja Peninsula this weekend.\nThere are indications that these two storm systems may interact\nwith each other, drawing atmospheric moisture northward. At this\ntime, low forecast confidence exists in this scenario. Timing,\ncoverage, and extent of any precipitation late this weekend into\nearly next week will need to be further refined in the upcoming \nfew days. \n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS: High forecast confidence in the\npossibility of a few strong thunderstorms in Eastern Nevada this\nafternoon into this evening. High forecast confidence regarding a\ncooling trend Tuesday and Wednesday. Low forecast confidence in\nperiods of rain this weekend into early next week.",
